			"updated": 1773380562758,
			"locked": false,
			"text": "Time in distributed systems is a mechanism used to order events and reason about causality\nacross multiple machines that do not share a global clock.\n\nThere are two fundamental types of time:\n\n1. Physical Time (Wall Clock Time)\n2. Logical Time (Event Ordering Time)",
			"rawText": "Time in distributed systems is a mechanism used to order events and reason about causality across multiple machines that do not share a global clock.\n\nThere are two fundamental types of time:\n\n1. Physical Time (Wall Clock Time)\n2. Logical Time (Event Ordering Time)",
			"fontSize": 20,
			"fontFamily": 8,
			"textAlign": "left",
			"verticalAlign": "middle",
			"containerId": "h044CLDT",
			"originalText": "Time in distributed systems is a mechanism used to order events and reason about causality across multiple machines that do not share a global clock.\n\nThere are two fundamental types of time:\n\n1. Physical Time (Wall Clock Time)\n2. Logical Time (Event Ordering Time)",
			"autoResize": true,
			"lineHeight": 1.25,
			"hasTextLink": false,
			"link": null
		},
		{
			"id": "VVn9ip96",
			"type": "rectangle",
			"x": 118.42012854820833,
			"y": 1424.1479921945065,
			"width": 462.849883915214,
			"height": 341.9592770297845,
			"angle": 0,
			"strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
